From a823dd1a4a2b3310b7dd2a664de234fbcdf8b69f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 14:05:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] Refuse concrete model pins on new delegations from
 Kanban workers

Problem: a managed Kanban worker could start new delegated work (delegate_task tasks, the subagent lifecycle service, an internal credentials_cfg route, a backend/default route) on a concrete model/provider, endpoint, credential or transport override. That recreates the per-model restriction the no-pin policy removes from cards, one level below the dispatcher.
Why it got through: model/provider selectors were only stripped as "hidden task fields" or passed straight into route resolution; nothing tied a child to the Kanban-origin parent that built it, so no boundary could tell a worker's delegation from an interactive one.
Why not fixed at one caller: there are several entry paths (registry handler, AIAgent dispatch, lifecycle service, route resolution, the child builder), and tier/runtime merges add request overrides after any early check.

Change (restriction only, grants no authority):
- agent/kanban_delegation_policy.py: pure logical checks plus a process-local weak identity map from live agent objects to a restriction token. The token is never serialized, read from the environment or accepted from a request field.
- agent/managed_worker.py registers the exact agent bound by WorkerCustody.bind_agent as a root; a natively bound worker with no registration refuses new delegation rather than falling back to interactive behaviour.
- tools/delegate_tool.py, run_agent.py: both model-facing strip callers preflight the original tasks before stripping; a valid class selector survives, any concrete model, provider, endpoint, credential or routing override refuses the whole batch before credential resolution.
- tools/delegate_tool_routes.py: Kanban-origin backend/default routes are staged to their logical class before any credential or profile resolution.
- agent/subagent_lifecycle.py validates the request before cleanup, correlation, credential, session or executor work.
- _build_child_agent checks the merged builder kwargs against the staged logical request as its first operation, registers each child with its parent's token, and request overrides are rechecked after the tier overlay.
Dynamic allocation inside AIAgent is unchanged: the concrete route is still chosen by the native allocator from the class. Interactive, cron and other unclassified parents keep the previous behaviour, including per-task pins where config allows them.

Proof (before publication, on base 9b201d02d4d1):
- Causal RED 3 failed / 5 passed before the late tier-merge fence, then GREEN. Focused run 224 passed, 0 failed: the three test files in this commit (no_pin 174, component79 24, lineage 8) plus an 18-case JSON-string task edge probe kept as card evidence, not committed.
- scripts/check: 21 checks OK.
- Importing-consumer sweep, 876 lexical module consumers, run per file, serial, retries 0, 3 GiB address-space limit, 8 GiB cgroup, Linux only. It is not 876 unconditional passes:
  - 349 files run with explicit MALLOC_ARENA_MAX=2 through the allocator opt-in landed separately in ad3b1eba4b: 347 green; tests/hermes_cli/test_code_attestation_deps.py failed once while the component was uncommitted (source-skew against HEAD) and passed 8/8 on the committed content; tests/hermes_cli/test_verify_console_scripts.py is a Windows-only whole-file skip, not applicable, not a pass.
  - The other 527 (462 + 60 + 5 excluded) were run earlier without the allocator opt-in; their recorded failures and exclusions are retained in the card evidence and are not converted to passes here. Required exact-head CI is the authority for those files.

Limits and unproved scope:
- Linux only. The real-builder positive test stubs child-session DB open and client creation: it proves fixture admission, class allocation and lineage, not a live model response.
- Not covered: auxiliary, MoA, plugin and external delegation paths outside delegate_task and the lifecycle service; the full per-sink override cross-product.
- This is the delegation boundary only. Card create/edit/set-model/review-handoff boundaries, fleet remediation, deployment and live admission proof are separate work on the card and are not claimed here.

